131 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 84 0 R
/E (Synthetic biology can help us make useful products in new ways. But a successful lab result is only one part of a much bigger system. Manufacturing needs energy. Biological feedstocks still use land and water. Engineered strains can change over generations. Products need to be recovered and purified. Labs need reliable supplies, and development needs appropriate oversight.)
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
132 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 84 0 R
/E (In SynBio Reality Check, the iGEM teams of IIT Delhi and IIT Bombay put these practical questions at the centre of two conversations. The first looks at manufacturing and environmental trade-offs through IIT Bombay's BiFROST data-centre coolant concept. The second turns to the wider conditions around synthetic biology: supply-chain dependence, variable waste streams, regulation and biosecurity.)
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
133 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 84 0 R
/E (The aim is not to dismiss the potential of synthetic biology. It is to make our claims more precise. Compare a product with a real alternative. Count the steps beyond the lab. Separate measured results from possible benefits. Be clear about what remains unknown.)
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
134 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 84 0 R
/E (A better project is not one with no unanswered questions. It is one that knows which questions still need evidence.)
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
135 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 84 0 R
/E (This booklet is an edited guide to the discussions, not a verbatim transcript or a claim that either project has demonstrated commercial-scale performance.)
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
