170 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 87 0 R
/E (Q. If a product is made using microorganisms, is it automatically lower-carbon?)
/Pg 15 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
171 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 87 0 R
/E (A. No. The manufacturing process matters. Fermentation can need substantial heat and electricity for aeration, agitation, sterilisation and cooling. The IIT Bombay team described using the ecoinvent database in an LCA of its coolant production route and noted that India's coal-heavy electricity mix can raise production-stage emissions.)
/Pg 15 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
172 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 87 0 R
/E (Q. What should a fair comparison include?)
/Pg 15 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
173 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 87 0 R
/E (A. It should include the inputs and energy needed to manufacture the product, the downstream steps needed to recover it, and relevant impacts during use. The comparison should use a defined conventional alternative and consistent system boundaries.)
/Pg 15 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
174 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 87 0 R
/E (Q. Could BiFROST still offer a benefit during use?)
/Pg 15 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
175 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 87 0 R
/E (A. Potentially. The team discussed a cyclic cooling loop: if the coolant improves thermal performance, the energy needed by pumps or chillers could fall over its lifetime. That is a benefit to test and quantify, not one to assume.)
/Pg 15 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
176 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 87 0 R
/E (REALITY CHECK)
/Pg 15 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
