/Pg 16 0 R
/K [0]
>>
endobj
182 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 88 0 R
/E (Q. Can switching from petroleum to glucose, cornstarch or sugarcane molasses create new problems?)
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
183 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 88 0 R
/E (A. Yes. These inputs can compete with other uses of agricultural land and water. Depending on how they are sourced and how production expands, land-use change and deforestation may also matter.)
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
184 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 88 0 R
/E (Q. Does that mean a bio-based product has no advantage?)
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
185 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 88 0 R
/E (A. Not necessarily. A bio-based route may reduce dependence on petroleum-derived inputs and exposure to oil-market or geopolitical shocks. But that advantage does not automatically cancel out the footprint of the feedstock or manufacturing process.)
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
186 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 88 0 R
/E (Q. What is the honest way to make the claim?)
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
187 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 88 0 R
/E (A. Compare against a real alternative, measure the relevant supply chain, and describe the result as a reduction only if the evidence supports it. Do not call a product carbon-neutral without evidence across the system being claimed.)
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
188 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 88 0 R
