194 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 89 0 R
/E (Q. Why might a strain that performs well in a short lab run lose productivity later?)
/Pg 17 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
195 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 89 0 R
/E (A. Producing a target compound can use energy and cellular resources. If the engineered pathway slows growth, variants that produce less may gain a reproductive advantage. A good initial result therefore does not guarantee stable output over many generations.)
/Pg 17 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
196 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 89 0 R
/E (Q. What did the IIT Bombay team identify as useful checks?)
/Pg 17 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
197 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 89 0 R
/E (A. Keep metabolic burden in mind during design, optimise fermentation conditions, and test the strain over multiple generations. The team also discussed Flux Balance Analysis \(FBA\) as a dry-lab tool to examine metabolic constraints and identify pathways that may be affected by an edit.)
/Pg 17 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
198 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 89 0 R
/E (Q. Can a genetic safeguard guarantee long-term stability?)
/Pg 17 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
199 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 89 0 R
/E (A. No single safeguard should be treated as infallible. Production stability, containment and performance under intended operating conditions need to be evaluated directly.)
/Pg 17 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
